                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Appearances can be decieving. For all its rough edges this is an unexpectedly deep point-and-click adventure game, designed in the older style. That is, if you can't connect the clues together in your own mind then chances are you'll be stuck.\n\nThis approach by the developers is both a blessing and a curse. When playing Dr. Doyle one realizes how streamlined other adventure games have become: hotspots and inventory are overly specific in their intent; without even having to think the player often knows that to do. Not so with Dr. Doyle. Hotspots are deliberately vague in their meaning, and they don't reveal useful information...unless the player can deduce the correct topic to use with it. Topics are what the game uses in place of inventory, and the difficulty comes from the fact that they're initially abstract. Exhausting every available topic onto every available hotspot doesn't always work, because topics must be cultivated and informed over time, and often in a certain order of deductive reasoning. In fact, the player can even cultivate misinformed topics, unknowingly leading to incorrect conclusions!\n\nThe game's strength is that it forces the player to intuit the clues themselves, in a [i]usually[/i] logical pattern, and this often leads to a genuine sense of discovery and satisfaction.\n\nThis results in a surprisingly deep point-and-click experience, at least when compared to any of its contemporaries. This also means a lot of frustration for the player, because it's slightly more idiot proof and random proof than what some of us have come to expect from the genre. There are also no walkthroughs available online (this isn't a very well known game).\n\nAs for the story, it's a [i]very[/i] dry English mystery. Old fashioned to the core. It's not my genre of storytelling, but the game does a surprisingly good job of telling it. I've completed a playthrough, yet I still haven't completed the full story, thus I don't even know the real ending. [i]I assume there are multiple endings?[/i] After a certain point in the game I unknowingly started to cultivate the wrong conclusions, which led to an ending in which I may have accused the wrong person.\n\nIt's presentation is very barebones, perhaps even crude. Main annoyances are the character's walk speed, and a kind of \"Simlish\" dubbing to all the voices. But I got over these flaws pretty early into the game.\n\nAll in all, this is a solid point-and-click that takes a riskier choice in its design, one that results in more frustration but more immersion.",
